WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:08.526
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.826 --> 00:00:16.645
Unit thirty-four: Ethics of Care. In this unit, you will evaluate the value of care in families and economies.

4
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.057
ethics of care.

5
00:00:19.757 --> 00:00:26.819
ethics of care means a moral theory that puts relationships and responsibility for others at the centre.

6
00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:31.214
The ethics of care asks not 'What is fair?' but 'Who needs me?'

7
00:00:31.914 --> 00:00:35.262
Your turn. Say: ethics of care.

8
00:00:37.962 --> 00:00:39.196
ethics of care.

9
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.219
caregiving.

10
00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:47.019
caregiving means looking after someone who is young, ill, elderly or disabled.

11
00:00:47.319 --> 00:00:50.526
Caregiving for her grandmother changed her career plans.

12
00:00:51.226 --> 00:00:53.692
Your turn. Say: caregiving.

13
00:00:56.392 --> 00:00:57.417
caregiving.

14
00:00:58.417 --> 00:00:59.766
emotional labour.

15
00:01:00.466 --> 00:01:05.751
emotional labour means the effort of managing feelings as part of a job or role.

16
00:01:06.051 --> 00:01:10.199
Nurses perform enormous emotional labour every shift.

17
00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:13.597
Your turn. Say: emotional labour.

18
00:01:16.297 --> 00:01:17.507
emotional labour.

19
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:19.796
care economy.

20
00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:24.876
care economy means all paid and unpaid work that looks after people.

21
00:01:25.176 --> 00:01:29.672
Investing in the care economy creates jobs and supports families.

22
00:01:30.372 --> 00:01:33.546
Your turn. Say: care economy.

23
00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:37.271
care economy.

24
00:01:38.283 --> 00:01:39.842
unpaid care work.

25
00:01:40.542 --> 00:01:45.525
unpaid care work means cooking, cleaning and caring for family without payment.

26
00:01:45.825 --> 00:01:49.067
Women still do most of the world's unpaid care work.

27
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:53.243
Your turn. Say: unpaid care work.

28
00:01:55.943 --> 00:01:57.397
unpaid care work.

29
00:01:58.383 --> 00:01:59.826
compassion fatigue.

30
00:02:00.526 --> 00:02:05.335
compassion fatigue means emotional exhaustion from caring for people who are suffering.

31
00:02:05.635 --> 00:02:09.318
Aid workers can suffer compassion fatigue after long missions.

32
00:02:10.018 --> 00:02:13.843
Your turn. Say: compassion fatigue.

33
00:02:16.543 --> 00:02:18.113
compassion fatigue.

34
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:20.710
relational ethics.

35
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.521
relational ethics means an approach to morality based on our connections with others.

36
00:02:26.821 --> 00:02:31.235
Relational ethics sees a person as part of a web of relationships.

37
00:02:31.935 --> 00:02:35.412
Your turn. Say: relational ethics.

38
00:02:38.112 --> 00:02:39.647
relational ethics.

39
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.767
dependency.

40
00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:45.872
dependency means the state of relying on others for support.

41
00:02:46.172 --> 00:02:50.122
Every human life begins and often ends in dependency.

42
00:02:50.822 --> 00:02:53.903
Your turn. Say: dependency.

43
00:02:56.603 --> 00:02:57.674
dependency.

44
00:02:58.683 --> 00:03:01.031
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:01.331 --> 00:03:06.872
Ms. Sarah, my mother cooks, cleans and cares for my grandmother, yet she says she 'doesn't work'.

46
00:03:08.183 --> 00:03:12.795
Unpaid though it is, that is real work, and economies depend on it.

47
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:16.036
Economists don't count it, do they?

48
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:24.368
Mostly not. Whereas a paid nurse adds to GDP, a daughter doing the same job adds nothing.

49
00:03:25.683 --> 00:03:27.207
That seems unfair.

50
00:03:28.517 --> 00:03:34.849
Granted, measuring care is difficult. Nevertheless, ignoring it makes millions of carers invisible.

51
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.219
And carers get exhausted too.

52
00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:45.640
Exactly. Much as we admire them, we rarely support them; compassion fatigue is the result.

53
00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:51.179
Grammar focus: Concession and contrast (extended).

54
00:03:51.479 --> 00:03:54.326
Concession makes an argument fair and convincing.

55
00:03:54.626 --> 00:04:02.537
Unpaid though it is, care is real work. Whereas a paid nurse adds to GDP, a daughter adds nothing.

56
00:04:02.837 --> 00:04:07.672
Granted, measuring care is difficult. Nevertheless, ignoring it is unjust.

57
00:04:07.972 --> 00:04:14.953
Strong arguments admit the other side first: 'granted', 'admittedly' and 'much as' prepare your counter-argument.

58
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.939
Now read and listen. The Invisible Economy.

59
00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:24.153
Every society depends on care: for children, for the sick and for the elderly.

60
00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:34.172
Philosophers such as Carol Gilligan and Nel Noddings developed the ethics of care as an alternative to theories based on abstract rules.

61
00:04:35.483 --> 00:04:43.648
Relational ethics reminds us that human beings are not isolated individuals; dependency is part of every life.

62
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:52.592
Yet the care economy is often undervalued, and unpaid care work is largely excluded from GDP.

63
00:04:53.883 --> 00:04:58.158
Globally, women perform around three quarters of this unpaid work.

64
00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:05.687
Paid caregiving jobs, meanwhile, involve heavy emotional labour but frequently offer low wages.

65
00:05:06.983 --> 00:05:14.138
Granted, care cannot be measured as easily as factory output; nevertheless, its value is immense.

66
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:21.525
Supporting carers, through fair pay, rest and recognition, also helps prevent compassion fatigue.

67
00:05:22.817 --> 00:05:25.492
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:29.696
Emotional exhaustion from caring for suffering people is blank.

69
00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:34.613
A, compassion fatigue. B, dependency. C, care economy.

70
00:05:38.617 --> 00:05:41.475
The answer is A: compassion fatigue.

71
00:05:42.983 --> 00:05:46.054
'blank though it is, care is real work.'

72
00:05:46.354 --> 00:05:50.402
A, Unpaid. B, Whereas. C, Granted.

73
00:05:54.417 --> 00:05:56.834
The answer is A: Unpaid.

74
00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:02.346
Which word introduces a direct contrast between two clauses?

75
00:06:02.646 --> 00:06:07.182
A, whereas. B, therefore. C, moreover.

76
00:06:11.183 --> 00:06:13.299
The answer is A: whereas.

77
00:06:14.783 --> 00:06:17.758
Cooking and caring for family without payment is blank.

78
00:06:18.058 --> 00:06:23.430
A, unpaid care work. B, emotional labour. C, relational ethics.

79
00:06:27.417 --> 00:06:30.299
The answer is A: unpaid care work.

80
00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:37.301
According to the text, roughly how much unpaid care work do women perform globally?

81
00:06:37.601 --> 00:06:42.253
A, about three quarters. B, about a quarter. C, about half.

82
00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:49.306
The answer is A: about three quarters.

83
00:06:50.817 --> 00:06:57.463
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should governments pay family members who care for elderly relatives?

84
00:07:02.763 --> 00:07:07.491
Part two: Concede one point against your view, then counter it.

85
00:07:13.791 --> 00:07:26.558
Admittedly, paying family carers would be expensive. Nevertheless, care is real work. Whereas nursing homes cost even more, supporting families keeps the elderly at home and prevents compassion fatigue.

86
00:07:26.858 --> 00:07:29.241
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:35.898
Great work today! You learned eight new words and concession and contrast (extended).

88
00:07:36.198 --> 00:07:40.499
Today's value is caring. Be gentle and kind to baby animals.

89
00:07:40.799 --> 00:07:44.473
See you in Unit thirty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:44.773 --> 00:07:47.495
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

